    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (BUILD SUCCESS, 1 test run \u2014 the only test class in the project, plus its supporting base/capture classes). \n\nThe implementation is complete. Here's what I did to satisfy the `customerCode` specification:\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `customerCode` field mapped to a new `customer_code` column, with getter/setter.\n\n2. **`AssignCustomerCode` function** (new, `rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`) \u2014 computes the code as `<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:\n   - `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of `lastName` (safely handles names shorter than 3),\n   - `NNNN` = `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, zero-padded to 4 digits (a global sequence = one more than the current owner count).\n\n3. **POST pipeline** (`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted the `assignCustomerCode` step between `build` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`, so the code is assigned before persistence and the count excludes the owner being created.\n\n4. **OpenAPI schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `customerCode` property to the `Owner` schema, so it's generated into `OwnerDto` and auto-mapped by MapStruct and returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n5. **Schema DDL** \u2014 added the `customer_code` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nThe acceptance test `coreFormatAndGlobalSequence` verifies `SMI-`/`JON-` prefixes and that the sequence increments by exactly 1 between consecutive creates \u2014 it passes, as does the full suite.",
